  "body": "Hi, I'm keeping having this genuine use case of a list inside a paragraph,\r\nbut the ul tag is a block level element and does not legally fit inside a paragraph.\r\n\r\nA simple example where this would be required would be (for instance): \r\n(tutorial tone)\r\nThe list that we want needs to be three things:\r\n- responsive\r\n- short and precise\r\n- colorful\r\n\r\nNow every time I come across this kind of use case (and due to my way of thinking and writing words, this happens a lot), I face the same problem: I don't want to break my paragraph, because to me it's part of the paragraph.\r\nI feel that I'm missing something if I wrap the sentence in a div instead of a p, because\r\nthat's supposed to be text after all, so I don't want to remove the textual quality out of my text, but yet I have no html tools to achieve this simple thing.\r\n\r\nI mean, I can certainly  use spans and css to achieve a visually pleasing list, but I think this is not just about me, this is more important than that, it's about having the html spec being\r\nsimple, complete and easy to use. \r\nIn a stack overflow post a long time ago I suggested an enum tag:\r\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/54440290/html5-enum-instead-of-ul-for-inline-lists-inside-paragraphs\r\n\r\nbut nobody cares. \r\nSo I'm hoping that an update on this post will generate more motivation posted here.\r\n\r\nIs it possible to create an enum tag (or equivalent)? \r\nIf not, do you deny that lists inside paragraph exist?\r\nIf not, what tools do we have to create inline lists?\r\n\r\nThanks, cheers.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
